stub: {"stubId": "testing-05", "topic": "testing", "slug": "wait-until-helper-silent-timeout", "level": "senior", "type": "find-bug", "subtopic": "testing async code", "angle": "A test of a background consumer polls with a WaitUntil helper and prints PASS although the consumer never processes the message; find the defective line in the helper.", "answerHinge": "The polling helper simply returns when the timeout elapses instead of failing, so the test passes without the condition ever becoming true; a wait-for-condition helper must throw or fail on timeout.", "sources": ["https://martinfowler.com/articles/nonDeterminism.html"]}
Generator notes: The explanation says Fowler's 'Eradicating Non-Determinism in Tests' recommends polling with a time limit instead of bare sleeps, and that the test should fail when the limit expires. I recall this from the 'Asynchronous Behavior' section, but the exact wording should be checked against the article.; Expected behaviour not executed by me: the original program prints PASS after about 500 ms. The fixed program ends with an unhandled System.TimeoutException, because top-level await rethrows the original exception rather than an AggregateException. The consumer's prefix mismatch ('orders:' vs 'order:42') makes this deterministic regardless of thread-pool timing. Please confirm with run-snippets.; Task.WaitAsync(TimeSpan) (available since .NET 6) is stated to throw TimeoutException when the timeout elapses; I believe this is correct.; I counted the defective line as line 19 (`return;` inside the deadline check) and the consumer prefix check as line 38 of code.source. Recount both when validating.
Mechanical evidence:
- executed in the sandbox; declared {"kind": "exception", "value": "System.TimeoutException"}; actual {"kind": "exception", "value": "System.TimeoutException"}
- warning: link unknown: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.timeoutexception (429)
Numbered code.source (quote every line listed in bugLineSets):
  1 | using System.Threading.Channels;
